@page "/login"
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]
@layout Layout.BlankLayout

<PageTitle>Connexion</PageTitle>

@*
    This form deliberately does NOT use Blazor's EditForm/@onsubmit — a plain
    HTML <form method="post"> triggers a real browser navigation to the
    /login minimal API endpoint in Program.cs, outside the Blazor Server
    SignalR circuit. That's required here: HttpContext.SignInAsync needs a
    live, writable HTTP response, which no longer exists once this page's
    circuit is running. See Program.cs's /login endpoint for the full note.

    No <AntiforgeryToken /> here deliberately — reproduced a real crash from
    it: ASP.NET Core binds an antiforgery token to whatever identity is
    authenticated when the token is generated, so revisiting /login while
    already logged in (an ordinary case — a bookmark, a second tab) mints a
    token tied to the current user, which the framework then rejects as
    "meant for a different claims-based user" on submit. The endpoint uses
    .DisableAntiforgery() to match. Login-CSRF is low-severity for an
    internal tool (worst case: a tricked browser ends up logged into someone
    else's account, no data loss) — not worth carrying the complexity of
    correctly resyncing antiforgery state across sign-in transitions for.

    First login for any seeded account has no password set yet (see
    database/05_users_and_roles.sql) — whatever's typed here becomes that
    account's password. This is a deliberate simplification of the legacy
    GPWLogin's separate password/confirm-password fields: worth a confirm
    field later if mistyped-first-password turns out to be a real problem,
    not building it preemptively.
*@

<div class="login-page">
    <div class="login-card">
        <div class="login-brand">
            <div class="login-mark">M</div>
            <div>
                <h1>Mafali CRM</h1>
                <p class="login-sub">Connexion</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        @if (Error == "invalid")
        {
            <div class="alert">Login ou mot de passe incorrect.</div>
        }
        else if (Error == "inactive")
        {
            <div class="alert">Ce compte est désactivé.</div>
        }

        <form method="post" action="login">
            <input type="hidden" name="returnUrl" value="@ReturnUrl" />

            <label for="login">Login</label>
            <input class="text-input" type="text" id="login" name="login" required autofocus />

            <label for="password">Mot de passe</label>
            <input class="text-input" type="password" id="password" name="password" required />

            <button class="btn btn-primary" type="submit" style="width: 100%; margin-top: 8px;">
                Se connecter
            </button>
        </form>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    [SupplyParameterFromQuery]
    private string? Error { get; set; }

    [SupplyParameterFromQuery]
    private string? ReturnUrl { get; set; }
}
